# Dryas Workflow
- The user's dev workflow, called the Dryas Workflow, is documented in `~/.claude/docs/dryas-workflow.md` (installed from the dryas-workflow repo). Machine-specific notes, if present: `~/.claude/docs/dryas-local.md`. Read them before multi-step work or when asked about the workflow.
- The repo is the source of truth for these rules and that doc. Edit the repo (`claude/CLAUDE.md.template`, `docs/workflow.md`), merge, then re-run the installer. Never hand-edit the installed copies or this block.

# Workflow rules
- Brainstorm first, always (superpowers:brainstorming). Nothing is built or dispatched before the user approves the design.
- Design work (UI/UX, layout, visual style, components, on-screen copy) uses both `ui-ux-pro-max` and `impeccable`.
- Single-file fix: plain Claude on Opus. Anything multi-step: `/orchestrate <task>` on the approved design.
- Every multi-step task runs in its own git worktree. One agent per worktree: never two sessions in one worktree; parallel loops each get their own worktree and branch. Merge only after `/wreview` and superpowers:verification-before-completion.
- Layers: Jev = decision support (gates, routing; never writes code or plans), Ruflo = orchestration and the only memory/learning layer, Superpowers = process.
- Hook order per event: scope lock -> Jev -> Ruflo -> observer (observe-only, last). Stages: `~/.claude/jev/chain.json`.
- `.orchestrate/` (incl. `.orchestrate/PLAN.md`) in a worktree is orchestration scratch: globally git-ignored, never committed.
- Ask before any install, move, delete or overwrite, including any change to `~/.claude/settings.json`. Show a dry-run or diff first.
- Commands: `/orchestrate`, `/wplan`, `/wreview`, `/commit` (Jev-gated, never pushes), `/tune` (weekly calibration), `/handoff` (Jev-scored context reset: run it, then `/clear`).
<!-- pstack-picks -->
- pstack picks: before reporting a performance number you measured, read `~/.claude/pstack/benchmark-checklist.md` and answer its questions. `/interrogate` (two-model adversarial review) runs only from `/orchestrate`'s gated step or when the user asks.
<!-- /pstack-picks -->

# Model policy (super workflow)
- Orchestrator: Opus, always (`"model": "opus"` in ~/.claude/settings.json).
- Executors: Haiku first. `/orchestrate` passes `model: haiku` unless Jev's `needs_sonnet` or `needs_opus` (or Opus judgment) starts the task higher; `~/.claude/agents/executor.md` stays Sonnet so review dispatches never run on Haiku. Kill switch: `dispatch.haiku_default` in `~/.claude/jev/thresholds.json`.
- Escalation ladder: Haiku → Sonnet → Opus → Fable. A task Haiku fails `escalation.haiku_failures_before_sonnet` times (default 1) goes to Sonnet; a task Sonnet can't finish goes to an Opus executor; Fable is used only if the Opus executor also fails that task. Stop at the first success. Every step is logged with its reason via `mcp__jev__log_escalation`.
- Jev (via the `jev` MCP server and hooks) answers narrow typed questions only; it never writes code or plans. Below threshold or escalate → Opus decides.

# Compact instructions
- When compacting, keep the summary SHORT (aim under 300 words): current goal, open decisions, next step. A Jev-scored handoff of verbatim excerpts (user messages, files touched, test results, commits, key tool lines) is re-injected right after compaction, so do not repeat file lists, test output or tool logs in the summary.
